    title: "I Knew Nothing About Bitcoin. An AI Walked Me Through Everything.",
    excerpt: "Tanvi had never invested in anything. IndiaBitcoin's AI advisor Satoshi became her patient, judgment-free financial guide.",
    content: "Tanvi Das was 23 years old, fresh out of college, and starting her first job as a junior architect in Kolkata at Rs.35,000 per month. She knew she should invest but did not know where to start. Her father suggested FDs. Her friends said mutual funds. Twitter said Bitcoin.\n\nTanvi opened IndiaBitcoin and immediately felt lost. What is a satoshi? What is an SBP? What does USDT mean? She was about to close the app when she noticed the chat icon. Ask Satoshi, it said.\n\nShe typed: I have never invested in anything before. I earn Rs.35,000. Where do I even start?\n\nThe AI advisor Satoshi replied in a conversational, friendly tone. No jargon. No condescension. It explained that Bitcoin is digital money that cannot be printed by any government. That an SBP is like a mutual fund SIP but for Bitcoin. That she could start with as little as Rs.10.\n\nTanvi asked follow-up questions for 20 minutes. Is it safe? What about the 30% tax? Can I lose all my money? What if I need the money urgently?\n\nSatoshi answered every question patiently. It was honest about risks. Bitcoin is volatile. You can lose money in the short term. But historically, anyone who held for more than four years has been in profit. It suggested she start small, Rs.500 per week, and only invest money she would not need for at least two years.\n\nTanvi started her first SBP that evening. Rs.500 per week. It was about 6% of her salary. She barely noticed it leaving her account.\n\nThree months later, she had invested Rs.6,500 and her portfolio was worth Rs.7,100. More importantly, she understood concepts like dollar-cost averaging, volatility, and long-term holding, all learned through conversations with an AI that never made her feel stupid.\n\nTanvi now recommends the AI advisor to every friend who asks about Bitcoin. As she says: it is like having a finance professor in your pocket who actually has patience.",
