    slug: "parivaar-wedding-fund-bitcoin",
    title: "We Are Saving for Our Daughter's Wedding in Bitcoin. Here Is Why.",
    excerpt: "The Singhs have 8 years before their daughter's wedding. They chose Bitcoin over gold for their wedding fund.",
    content: "Harpreet and Jasmine Singh from Chandigarh have a 14-year-old daughter, Simran. In Punjab, weddings are elaborate affairs. They estimate they will need Rs.25-30 lakh in about 8 years.\n\nTraditionally, the family would have bought gold. Harpreet's parents saved for his wedding in gold. But gold has returned about 10% annually over the last decade. At that rate, they would need to save about Rs.15,000 per month for 8 years to reach Rs.25 lakh.\n\nHarpreet did the math on Bitcoin. If Bitcoin continues to return even 30% annually (well below its historical average of 100%+), a monthly investment of Rs.5,000 could potentially reach Rs.25 lakh in 8 years. That is one-third the monthly commitment.\n\nThe family started a Parivaar Portfolio on IndiaBitcoin. Harpreet contributes Rs.3,000 per month. Jasmine adds Rs.2,000. Simran's grandmother sends Rs.500 from her pension. Total: Rs.5,500 per month into a family Bitcoin SBP.\n\nThe Parivaar feature lets each family member see their contribution and the collective total. They have set a family goal of Rs.25,00,000 with a target date of 2034. The progress bar shows them exactly where they stand.\n\nAfter six months, the family vault has Rs.38,000 invested and is worth Rs.44,500. They are ahead of schedule.\n\nHarpreet is realistic about the risks. Bitcoin could crash 50% in any given year. That is why they still maintain a gold SIP and a fixed deposit as backup. The Bitcoin fund is their moonshot. If it works, Simran gets the wedding of her dreams. If it does not, the gold and FD still cover a respectable celebration.\n\nThe family discusses their Bitcoin portfolio every Sunday over lunch. Simran has started learning about Bitcoin herself. She says she might ask for Bitcoin as a wedding gift instead of gold.",
