    slug: "lightning-network-chai-wala-payments",
    title: "A Chai Wala in Varanasi Now Accepts Lightning Payments. Seriously.",
    excerpt: "Manoj runs a tea stall near the ghats. His tourist customers pay in Bitcoin via Lightning. He receives INR instantly.",
    content: "Manoj Tiwari runs a chai stall near Dashashwamedh Ghat in Varanasi. On a good day, he serves 200 cups of chai to a mix of locals, domestic tourists, and foreign visitors. The foreigners always had the same problem: they did not have Indian rupees and UPI did not work with their foreign bank accounts.\n\nManoj used to lose 10-15 sales per day because of this. At Rs.20 per chai, that was Rs.200-300 per day or about Rs.6,000-9,000 per month in lost revenue.\n\nHis nephew Amit, a computer science student at BHU, set up a Lightning payment option for Manoj's stall. The setup took 20 minutes. IndiaBitcoin provided a QR code that customers could scan with any Lightning wallet.\n\nWhen a tourist pays in Bitcoin via Lightning, the payment arrives in under 3 seconds. IndiaBitcoin's Auto Sell feature converts it to INR instantly at zero fees. Manoj sees rupees, not Bitcoin. He does not need to understand cryptocurrency. He just needs to show the QR code.\n\nThe first week, Manoj received seven Lightning payments from tourists. A backpacker from Brazil paid Rs.40 for two cups. A German couple paid Rs.100 for chai and biscuits. A Bitcoin conference attendee from Bangalore paid Rs.20 for a single cup and left a Rs.200 tip in Bitcoin because he was so excited to use Lightning at a chai stall.\n\nWord spread among the tourist community in Varanasi. Manoj's stall became a minor attraction. Tourists would specifically visit to pay for chai with Bitcoin. His daily revenue went up 15%.\n\nManoj now has a small sign next to his price list: Lightning Accepted Here. He still does not fully understand Bitcoin. But he understands that the QR code brings him customers he would otherwise lose.\n\nHis nephew Amit has since set up Lightning payments for three other vendors near the ghats. He calls it the Lightning Ghat Initiative.",
